WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.660
Mina runs a fictional dispatch library.

2
00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:08.691
Her team just added AI helpers, and they
can all prepare work at the same time.

3
00:00:08.831 --> 00:00:12.191
Each helper writes its own
labelled working version.

4
00:00:12.191 --> 00:00:15.941
But to file anything, they all
need the same oversized key,

5
00:00:15.941 --> 00:00:18.266
so one card goes in at a time.

6
00:00:18.406 --> 00:00:21.676
Add more helpers, and the
lock doesn’t open any faster.

7
00:00:21.676 --> 00:00:23.225
The queue just grows.

8
00:00:23.365 --> 00:00:27.245
In software, a repository
stores a project’s versions.

9
00:00:27.245 --> 00:00:31.775
A branch, or reference, is a named
pointer to one of those versions.

10
00:00:31.775 --> 00:00:34.522
Mina’s library is our analogy for that.

11
00:00:34.662 --> 00:00:39.062
GitHub’s engineers describe a similar
bottleneck in their Git storage.

12
00:00:39.062 --> 00:00:42.270
Writes to a repository
were ordered as one,

13
00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:46.838
and each write waited for several round
trips between distant data centres.

14
00:00:46.978 --> 00:00:51.388
GitHub says the shift toward
AI agents shaped this work.

15
00:00:51.388 --> 00:00:54.858
Its redesign moves the ordering
to individual references,

16
00:00:54.858 --> 00:00:59.185
so updates to unrelated references
can proceed independently.

17
00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:03.200
In Mina’s library, each
independent shelf gets its own key.

18
00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.123
Helpers working on different
shelves stop waiting for each other.

19
00:01:07.263 --> 00:01:11.643
But two cards for the same shelf still
pass one numbered gate, in order,

20
00:01:11.643 --> 00:01:14.970
so nobody sees an older
version after a newer one.

21
00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:19.810
GitHub presents this as evolving
architecture, with internal tests.

22
00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.452
It isn’t a promise that every
team’s agents will run faster.

23
00:01:23.592 --> 00:01:26.452
So before you add more
agents, map the work.

24
00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:28.470
What truly has to happen in order?

25
00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:30.090
What can run on its own?

26
00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:33.424
Then measure the whole workflow,
not the number of helpers.

27
00:01:33.564 --> 00:01:37.254
More helpers only help when the
work isn’t waiting on one key.

28
00:01:37.394 --> 00:01:38.864
Ahmadreza Samadi.

29
